    purpose: 'Give attention position information without adding a learned position vector to the residual stream, in a form that decays with distance and that has a clean relative-position property.',
    mechanics: "Within each head, the `d_head` dimensions are grouped into `d_head / 2` pairs; pair `i` has frequency `θ_i = base^(−2i / d_head)`, with `base` (often called `rope_theta`) commonly 10,000 and larger in long-context models (Llama 3 uses 500,000). A token at position `m` has its pair `i` rotated by angle `m × θ_i`: `(x_{2i}, x_{2i+1}) → (x_{2i} cos(mθ_i) − x_{2i+1} sin(mθ_i), x_{2i} sin(mθ_i) + x_{2i+1} cos(mθ_i))`. The rotation is applied to Q and K after their projections and before the dot product; V is not rotated. Then `⟨R_m q, R_n k⟩ = ⟨q, R_{n−m} k⟩`, so the score depends on `n − m` only. Low-index pairs rotate fast (local, fine-grained positions); high-index pairs rotate slowly (coarse, long-range). Extending context beyond the trained length without retraining is handled by scaling strategies: position interpolation compresses positions into the trained range, NTK-aware scaling raises `base` so that high frequencies keep resolution while low frequencies stretch, and YaRN combines per-frequency-band interpolation with an attention temperature adjustment; these are commonly used with a short fine-tuning phase, and their effect is model dependent. Implementations precompute `cos` and `sin` tables `[T_max, d_head / 2]` once and reuse them; with document masking in packed training, positions restart per document.",
