    mechanics: "For layer `ℓ` in a pre-norm decoder block: `h_ℓ = x_ℓ + Attn_ℓ(RMSNorm(x_ℓ))` and `x_{ℓ+1} = h_ℓ + MLP_ℓ(RMSNorm(h_ℓ))`. The stream has width `d_model` at every layer; sublayers project out to their own widths (`n_heads × d_head` for attention, `d_ff` for the MLP) and project back to `d_model` before adding. The final residual `x_L` is normalized and sent to the LM head. Because updates are additive, the norm of the stream tends to grow with depth, which is one reason pre-norm (normalize the branch input) is preferred over post-norm for deep stacks. In an MoE model the MLP branch writes the weighted sum of expert outputs into the same stream; the residual structure does not change. Activation memory per layer is dominated by tensors on this path and the branch intermediates saved for backward.",
